#README

Website URL : cheerleadingsg.com

📣 SG Cheer & Gym Collective: Project Documentation
1. Project Goals & Vision
The primary objective of this project is to centralize the fragmented Singaporean cheerleading and gymnastics market into a single, high-performance digital ecosystem.

Community Hub: Create a "digital home" for athletes to consume high-quality local content.

Conversion Excellence: Streamline the transition from "viewer" to "student" through a frictionless booking and payment interface.

Social Validation: Build a transparent trust economy via verified student feedback and video testimonials.

Operational Efficiency: Automate administrative tasks—such as waiver collection, class scheduling, and payment processing—allowing coaches to focus on training.

Scalable Architecture: Develop a platform capable of handling seasonal traffic spikes (e.g., during national competition periods).

2. The Singapore Cheerleading Scene
Cheerleading in Singapore has evolved from a niche CCA (Co-Curricular Activity) into a highly competitive, internationally recognized sport.

Landscape: The scene is characterized by a mix of school-based teams (Secondary and Tertiary) and "All-Star" (private) clubs. Major events like the National Cheerleading Championships (NCC) and Charity Cheerleading competitions drive the community's energy.

The Shift: There is a growing trend toward "Tumbling" and "Acrobatics" as standalone fitness disciplines. Many adults in Singapore are now seeking gymnastics-based conditioning as a fun alternative to traditional gyms.

Market Gap: While the talent is high, the digital infrastructure for booking and viewing high-quality local media is often outdated. This project fills that void by providing a modern, "lifestyle-brand" feel to the sport.

3. AWS Cloud Integration & Architecture
To ensure high availability, low latency (crucial for video playback), and secure payments, the platform utilizes a robust AWS stack.

Core Infrastructure
Frontend Hosting: AWS Amplify or S3 + CloudFront. Using CloudFront ensures that video content is cached at edge locations in Singapore, providing near-instant playback for local users.

API & Backend: AWS AppSync (GraphQL) or Amazon API Gateway with AWS Lambda. This "serverless" approach allows the site to scale automatically during high-traffic registration windows.

Database: Amazon DynamoDB for low-latency retrieval of class schedules, user profiles, and review data.

Media & Storage
Video Processing: AWS Elemental MediaConvert. When a coach uploads a 4K performance video, MediaConvert automatically transcodes it into multiple resolutions (1080p, 720p, 480p) to ensure smooth streaming on varied mobile data speeds in Singapore.

Object Storage: Amazon S3 stores all raw media, student waivers, and profile images.

Security & Payments
Authentication: Amazon Cognito handles secure user sign-ups, social logins (Google/Apple), and multi-factor authentication.

Secret Management: AWS Secrets Manager securely stores API keys for payment gateways like Stripe or PayNow.

4. Learning Lessons & Development Insights
Developing a specialized sports platform yields several critical takeaways:

User Experience (UX) Psychology
Visual Trust: In high-risk sports like cheerleading, "seeing is believing." High-definition video of safe, successful stunts is more effective at driving sign-ups than any marketing copy.

The "Three-Click" Rule: For the Singaporean market, where users value efficiency, the journey from landing on the site to receiving a booking confirmation must be completed in under three clicks.

Technical Challenges
Video Optimization: Managing the storage costs and delivery speeds of high-resolution video is the biggest technical hurdle. Implementing a Content Delivery Network (CDN) is non-negotiable.

Concurrency Management: During "Early Bird" class releases, hundreds of users may attempt to book the same 15 spots simultaneously. Using DynamoDB Transactional Writes is essential to prevent overbooking.

Business Logic
Segmentation: Separating "Cheer" and "Gym" is vital. While the skill sets overlap, the intent of the users is different. A gymnast may not want to see cheer pom-poms, and a cheerleader needs a specific focus on stunting. Tailoring the UI for these two personas increases overall retention.

5. Future Roadmap
AI Coach: Integration of computer vision to allow students to upload videos of their "back tucks" for automated form feedback.

E-commerce Expansion: An AWS-backed storefront for localized "SG Cheer" merchandise.

Live Streaming: Using Amazon IVS (Interactive Video Service) to live-stream local competitions directly on the platform.
